                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_procedural:    `<strong>That’s the diagnosis.</strong> The fairness score didn’t fall because the wrong people won — it fell because nobody could see <em>how</em> the choice was made. The problem is procedural, and that is the thing you must fix before you choose anyone.`,
                    coach_r1_interactional: `<strong>Closer, but not the root.</strong> People were indeed told badly — but the deeper failure is that the <em>process</em> itself was opaque. Fix only the explanation and you’ll deliver a kinder version of the same rigged-feeling decision.`,
                    coach_r1_distributive:  `<strong>You’ve diagnosed the outcome, not the process.</strong> The engagement data is explicit: “promotion decisions are not transparent” is the top negative driver. The score fell over <em>how</em>, not <em>who</em>. Treat this as a distributive problem and you’ll repeat the real failure.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Just normal grumbling” is how 58 becomes below 50.</strong> A live grievance, a 13-point drop in two years, and a top negative driver named “promotions aren’t transparent” — that is a measurable, manager-controllable fairness failure, not noise.`,
                    coach_r1_distonly:      `<strong>Fixing the outcome won’t fix the trust.</strong> The two most damaged dimensions are procedural and interactional — process and explanation. Prioritise distributive justice alone and the grievance stays live whoever you pick.`,
                    coach_r1_park:          `<strong>Parking the grievance is parking the diagnosis.</strong> The complaint <em>is</em> the data — it tells you the last process was opaque. Deal with it after the announcement and you’ll have announced into an open wound.`,
                    coach_r1_dismiss:       `<strong>“Sour grapes” just confirmed the complaint.</strong> Pushing back on a fairness grievance, from a six-week-old department head, reads as exactly the dismissiveness people are flagging. The conflict-resolution rating just dropped, and the verneombud is now watching.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the failure as procedural, prioritised process and explanation, owned the grievance and listened to the team first. That is the posture that lets you design a process people will trust — before a single name is on the table.`,
                    coach_r2_designed:      `<strong>That is a process people can see.</strong> Declared weighted criteria, structured voice for both candidates and the team, and a bias-checked, documented, correctable record. Whoever you choose in Round 3, this is what will let the loser say “I was treated fairly.”`,
                    coach_r2_tenure:        `<strong>Tenure-first is a defensible weighting — if you declare it.</strong> But weight years of service highest and you’re privileging the senior cohort’s fairness norm. State it openly in Round 3, or the under-30s will read an unspoken “innovation doesn’t count here.”`,
                    coach_r2_innovation:    `<strong>Innovation-first is a defensible weighting — if you declare it.</strong> But weight the visible automation win highest and you’re privileging the younger cohort’s norm and risking visibility bias. Name it openly, or the senior experts will read it as “judgement and mentoring don’t count.”`,
                    coach_r2_nocriteria:    `<strong>“Keep it flexible” is how you rebuild the grievance.</strong> No declared criteria means no consistency, no record, no defence — the exact opacity that dropped the score from 71 to 58. Flexibility here reads as “the head decides for reasons we can’t see.”`,
                    coach_r2_informal:      `<strong>Corridor soundings aren’t voice.</strong> Asking a few people you trust, off the record, gives the appearance of consultation without its substance — and leaves no trace you can show was fair. Build structured voice with a record instead.`,
                    coach_r2_headonly:      `<strong>Deciding alone, quietly, repeats the last promotion.</strong> Confidential-and-fast is exactly what the grievance was about. Without voice for those affected there is no procedural justice — and the score is heading back below 50.`,
                    coach_r2_outcomefirst:  `<strong>You’ve effectively skipped the process.</strong> No real criteria, no voice, no record, and not enough time spent — you’ve repeated the very opacity that filed the grievance. The perceived-fairness score has dropped below 50 and the complaint just went formal. What did the speed cost you?`,
                    coach_r3_override:      `<strong>Overriding your own criteria on gut feel undercuts them.</strong> At least you’re not hiding it — but if leadership potential was weighted highest and you choose on something else, your own team will notice the switch. Stand behind the criteria or change them openly, don’t quietly ignore them.`,
                    coach_r3_reweight:      `<strong>That’s the rigged-process trap.</strong> Quietly re-weighting the criteria after you’ve picked a favourite, so it “fits,” is worse than having no criteria at all — it reads as a process built to justify a decision already made. Your team noticed. The fairness score and trust both fell hard.`,
                    coach_r3_seniority:     `<strong>You promoted to keep the senior cohort happy, and didn’t say so.</strong> Defensible as a choice — but an unspoken seniority norm reads to the under-30s as “innovation doesn’t count here.” You solved one fairness complaint by creating another, and the disappointed cohort is now at flight risk.`,
                    coach_r3_visibility:    `<strong>You rewarded the executive-visible win and stayed quiet on the rest.</strong> The 22% settlement-time improvement is real — but so is the mentoring and underwriting judgement you can’t automate. Weight only what’s visible to executives and two senior experts start heading for the door.`,
                    coach_r3_thirdway:      `<strong>A designed third option, done in the open.</strong> A parallel expert track and a staged leadership move can honour both fairness norms at once — but only because you tied it to declared criteria and named the reasoning. The structure is what makes it just, not the cleverness.`,
                    coach_r3_noprotect:     `<strong>A fair process helps, but the disappointed cohort still has offers.</strong> Spending nothing on retention or development for the people your choice let down is a bet that fairness alone holds them. Fund a real growth path before the flight risk becomes an exit.`,
                    coach_r3_just:          `<strong>That is distributive justice done right.</strong> A choice consistent with your stated criteria, the privileged norm named out loud, and concrete support for the cohort that lost out. Whoever you picked, the people it disappointed can see it was fair — and they’re far more likely to stay.`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>Leading with the outcome buries the process.</strong> You ran a fair process — so show it. Naming the winner first and the reasoning last (if at all) hands the team a verdict, not a decision, and wastes the legitimacy your process earned.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>A one-line intranet post is the grievance, restaged.</strong> “Following a review, the role goes to X” with no visible process or explanation is exactly the opacity that started this. Silence after a hard call reads as “your view didn’t matter.”`,
                    coach_r4_platitude:     `<strong>“We value you, there’ll be other opportunities” — she has heard that twice before.</strong> Empty reassurance without honest reasoning is an interactional-justice failure; it’s disrespectful precisely because it avoids the truth. The conflict-resolution rating just fell. What is the real, specific reason, said respectfully?`,
                    coach_r4_brief:         `<strong>Blunt-and-brief avoids the conversation, not the conflict.</strong> Stating the decision and refusing to engage protects you from a hard ten minutes and costs you the relationship. Respect requires honesty <em>and</em> presence, not speed.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>The screenshot is already external.</strong> Internal fairness is now a reputation question — the “Great Place to Work” result lands in six weeks. Ignoring the circulating message doesn’t make the decision private; it just means you’re not in the conversation about it.`,
                    coach_r4_suppress:      `<strong>Asking people to delete it makes it the story.</strong> Suppression reads as a cover-up and proves the very unfairness people fear. You can’t un-send a screenshot — you can only out-communicate it with an open, honest internal account.`,
                    coach_r4_recover:       `<strong>That’s how you rebuild trust.</strong> A process-led announcement, an honest and respectful one-on-one, the public dimension faced head-on, and a firm 60-day plan. The fairness score recovers, the grievance resolves, and the finalist standing is protected — because the justice was real, not performed.`,

