                    insight_just:           `Justice restored: you diagnosed a procedural failure, designed a visible and consistent process, made a choice you could tie to declared criteria, named whose norm it privileged, and explained it honestly and respectfully. The perceived-fairness score recovered, the grievance resolved, the at-risk experts stayed, and the “Great Place to Work” standing held — because the justice was real, not performed.`,
                    insight_rigged:         `Rigged process: quietly re-weighting the criteria after picking a favourite read as a process built to justify a decision already made — worse than having no criteria at all. Your own team noticed the switch, fairness and trust fell hard, and the grievance escalated. The candidate may even have been right; the process poisoned it.`,
                    insight_outcomefirst:   `Outcome before process: you picked a candidate without a real, visible procedure — and repeated the very opacity that filed the grievance. The perceived-fairness score fell below 50 and the complaint went formal. A fair process is precisely what makes an unfavourable outcome acceptable; without it, even a defensible pick is read as a verdict.`,
                    insight_platitude:      `Interactional failure: an honest, respectful explanation is the difference between “I disagree but I was treated fairly” and a grievance. Empty reassurance to the candidate who lost was disrespectful precisely because it avoided the truth — the conflict-resolution rating never recovered.`,
                    insight_seniority:      `Conflict avoided, conflict created: choosing to placate the senior cohort without naming the norm read to the under-30s as “innovation doesn’t count here.” Every distributive choice has a counterparty — and the cohort you disappointed went to elevated flight risk.`,
                    insight_visibility:     `Visibility bias: over-rewarding the executive-visible automation win under-valued the mentoring and underwriting judgement you can’t automate. Two senior experts began to disengage, and irreplaceable knowledge started walking toward the door.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: parts of the justice work landed, but the diagnosis, process, choice and explanation didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough seams for the fairness conflict to persist and for the team to keep watching.`,
                    insight_formal:         `The perceived-fairness score fell below 50: in the model, the grievance escalates to a formal HR/union (verneombud) process from here. Recovery is possible, but the decision is now contested ground, not a closed file.`,
                    insight_retention:      `You lost flight-risk experts: each departure costs 600 000–900 000 kr in replacement and lost productivity, and the senior experts take underwriting judgement that doesn’t come back. Fairness, trust and conflict resolution were the leading indicators that would have warned you in time.`,
                    insight_reputation:     `You let the reputational dimension run unmanaged: a single promotion decision became an external “Great Place to Work” question. The fairness heuristic means employees — and now the award panel — read this one decision as a signal about the whole culture.`,
                    insight_counterparty:   `Fair on paper, costly in people: the process and explanation were sound, but the cohort your choice disappointed still left. A distributive decision always has a counterparty — protecting and developing them is part of the justice, not an afterthought.`,

                    hint_inconsistent:      `You re-weighted the criteria to fit your pick — that reads as a rigged process and it’s capping your fairness and trust. The only repair is to stand behind the declared criteria openly, or change them in the open and explain why.`,
                    hint_outcomefirst:      `You skipped the process and the grievance went formal. A fair, visible procedure is what makes an unfavourable outcome acceptable — rebuild voice, criteria and an honest explanation before more trust is lost.`,
                    hint_formal:            `Perceived fairness is below 50 — the grievance is now a formal verneombud process. Lead the next round with visible process and honest, respectful explanation to pull it back.`,
                    hint_platitude:         `Platitudes to the candidate who lost were an interactional-justice failure. The repair is honesty: the real, specific reason, said with respect, and a concrete next step you actually commit to.`,
                    hint_reputation:        `Reputational exposure is high — the circulating screenshot has made this decision effectively public with the award six weeks out. Address it head-on internally rather than hoping it stays private.`,
                    hint_retention:         `You’re losing flight-risk experts. Raise fairness and conflict resolution, name the privileged norm, and fund a real growth path for the cohort your choice disappointed.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — fairness, trust and conflict resolution are all healthy. Land it: a process-led announcement, an honest one-on-one and a firm 60-day plan will hold it through the award.`,

